# F and Φ, resolved all the way to a layer that is actually here. One row per part whose notation
# names a document in this corpus and whose layer is in that document.
#
# One resolution, written once
#   Every question about composition (convert the parts, walk the descent, find a leaf, attenuate
#   a coupling) starts by turning a notation into a filing and a filing plus a layer into a row.
#   Written once, the resolution rule is one thing that can be corrected in one place.
#
# A missing row is a dangling reference
#   A row present in composition/part_references.sqlc and missing from here is a dangling
#   reference, and the difference between the two relations is exactly the violation set of that
#   rule. The rule is an anti-join rather than a `NOT EXISTS`, because "the parts that resolve" is
#   the relation worth naming and "the parts that do not" is its complement.
#
# Resolution is a reader's assertion one step removed
#   `filing_identity` says which document claims which notation, and `asserted_by` records who
#   said it. Filled in as "the reader, from the filename", that would be a guess dressed as data;
#   these are read out of the documents.
#
# `pm.layer` directly, not `layers/every_layer.sqlc`
#   The last join is a membership test: does the layer this reference names exist.
#   `layers/every_layer.sqlc` is the layer dimension, reserved for denominators. Composed here it
#   would drag the entire dimension into the transitive closure of most of the checker's rules,
#   and any reach-containment law over a rule is vacuous the moment the dimension is inside its
#   closure, because the dimension reaches everything by construction. `algebra/dimension_use.sqlc`
#   states the rule and enforces it over the compose DAG.
-- pm.part joined through pm.filing_identity to pm.layer.
SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer,
       p.part_filing AS part_notation,
       fi.filing     AS part_filing,
       p.part_layer,
       p.part_regime,
       p.factor_low, p.factor_mode, p.factor_high, p.factor_absent, p.factor_derivation,
       p.factor_state
FROM      (
    :compose(composition/part_references.sqlc)
) p
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/notations.sqlc)
) fi ON fi.notation = p.part_filing
JOIN pm.layer l  ON l.filing = fi.filing AND l.layer = p.part_layer
